-- MEASURE THE BRIDGE: can we get IsSemisimpleModule A V where A is the subalgebra of End ImO
-- generated by an ideal I's action? The key: V=ImO is a finite-dim ℚ-space; the I-action lands
-- in skew-adjoint operators; Born-orthogonality gives complemented lattice of A-submodules.
-- An A-submodule of ImO = an I-invariant subspace = (since elements of I are derivations) a
-- subspace closed under the I-action. Its gBil-orthogonal complement is also I-invariant
-- (deriv_mapsTo_orthogonal applies to each element of I). So the lattice of A-submodules is
-- complemented ⟹ IsSemisimpleModule A V.

-- First: is the relevant subalgebra A even easy to form? Probe the type of the I-action on ImO.
-- imRep : derivationLieQ →ₗ⁅ℚ⁆ Module.End ℚ ImO. For an ideal I, restrict imRep to I.

-- The cleanest abstract bridge: a set S of skew-adjoint (for a definite form) operators on a
-- finite-dim space V gives a semisimple action. Mathlib's IsSemisimpleModule is over a RING A.
-- We'd need A = Algebra.adjoin ℚ (imRep '' I) and V as an A-module. That's the heavy infra.

-- MEASURE: instead, can the isotypic STEP A be reframed WITHOUT building A as a ring?
-- The real content: V has no proper I-invariant subspace W with a DIFFERENT I-structure on the
-- complement... Actually STEP A is exactly: the I-action on V is isotypic. The cleanest Mathlib
-- route requires the associative module. Let me measure if building the A-module is heavy.

-- Probe: form the ℚ-subalgebra of End ℚ ImO generated by the range of (imRep ∘ I.incl).
example (I : LieIdeal ℚ derivationLieQ) :
    Subalgebra ℚ (Module.End ℚ ImO) :=
  Algebra.adjoin ℚ (Set.range (fun x : I => imRep (I.incl x)))

-- Probe: is V=ImO an A-module automatically (it's an End-module, A ⊆ End)?
example (I : LieIdeal ℚ derivationLieQ)
    (A : Subalgebra ℚ (Module.End ℚ ImO)) : Module A ImO := by
  exact?

end
